    "contact_source_ids": ["s1"],
    "description": "Iran’s Ministry of Information and Communications Technology has a documented remit covering postal services, telephones and information technology. Its development responsibilities intersect with other institutions’ authority, making infrastructure provision, sector coordination and restrictions on internet access distinct subjects.",
    "tags": ["ICT", "Telecommunications", "Internet governance", "Postal services", "Digital infrastructure"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T23:29:04Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T23:29:04Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Iran’s Ministry of ICT: Roles and Governance | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore the ministry’s communications remit, information technology institutions and shared governance responsibilities, with dated evidence."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "communications-remit",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From postal and telephone services to ICT",
        "content": "ICT stands for information and communications technology: the networks, systems and services through which information is transmitted and used. The United Nations Institute for Disarmament Research, or UNIDIR, describes Iran’s ICT ministry as responsible for postal services, telephones and information technology. Its institutional record identifies 2003 as the year the ministry was renamed and its role codified. This should not be mistaken for the beginning of Iran’s postal or telephone administration. [s1]\n\nUNESCO’s education-policy profile explains that the 2003 legislation transferred the duties and responsibilities of the former Ministry of Post, Telegraph and Telephone to the renamed ministry. The change therefore combined institutional continuity with an expanded technological vocabulary. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "information-technology-organization",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "An institution within the information technology portfolio",
        "content": "A historical profile hosted by the United Nations’ Asian and Pacific Training Centre for ICT for Development documents the evolution of Iran’s Information Technology Organization, or ITO. It records the separation of the Information Technology Company from the Telecommunication Company of Iran in 2007, placing it under direct ministry control, and its renaming as ITO in 2011. [s3]\n\nThe profile describes objectives including strategic IT management, infrastructure continuity and broader access to information technology. As an institutional presentation from 2013, it establishes how the organization described its role at that time. It does not independently demonstrate service quality, equal access or the present distribution of responsibilities. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "shared-policy-responsibilities",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Digital services require cooperation across institutions",
        "content": "Education provides a concrete example of overlapping responsibilities. UNESCO’s profile describes school technology projects involving both the education and communications ministries, with approvals from the Supreme Council of Cyberspace. It also distinguishes the communications ministry’s infrastructure contribution from the education ministry’s responsibility for public education platforms. [s2]\n\nThis division is important for interpreting announcements about digital government or connected public services. Providing a network, managing a platform and deciding its educational purpose are different functions. The education example illustrates a shared arrangement; it should not be treated as a complete organizational map for every digital service. The UNESCO profile is archived and was last modified in January 2025, so its account requires updating before use as a current administrative guide. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "access-and-restrictions",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Connectivity and freedom to communicate",
        "content": "Infrastructure expansion alone does not establish unrestricted access to information. Freedom House’s 2025 Iran report documented extensive filtering and restrictions, alongside a nationwide shutdown in June 2025. It reported the communications ministry’s explanation that the shutdown was temporary and connected to wartime conditions. The report also attributed the December 2024 decision to lift restrictions on WhatsApp and Google Play to the Supreme Council for Cyberspace. [s4]\n\nThese examples distinguish the ministry’s public statements and communications responsibilities from decisions involving other authorities. They do not establish that the ministry alone ordered every restriction. A complete account through October 2026 needs updated evidence about decision-making authority, implementation and the experience of users across the country.",
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      }
    ],
